window.NOTES.push(
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Measurement',
    qt: ['Units and measurement'],
    title: 'Units, dimensions and significant figures',
    body: 'Measurement questions open nearly every Physics paper, because every later topic depends on a number being attached to the right unit and the right number of figures. Units, dimensions, scalars and vectors, significant figures and simple unit conversions all come from the first section of the examination and can be answered in seconds once you know what to look for.\n\nHere\'s the idea: a physical quantity is only a number once you have fixed three things, namely its unit, its dimension and how precisely it is stated, and every unit in the syllabus is either one of the seven base units or built out of them.\n\nStep 1 - Fix the unit from the quantity: metre for length, kilogram for mass, second for time, newton for force, joule for work, watt for power, pascal for pressure and hertz for frequency.\nStep 2 - Fix the dimension by writing length as L, mass as M and time as T, so force is MLT^-2 and work is ML^2T^-2; if the two sides of an equation do not match dimensionally, the equation is wrong.\nStep 3 - Count significant figures carefully: leading zeros never count, so 0.00450 has three, and round rather than chop when a question asks you to correct a value.\nStep 4 - Convert units before substituting, using 1 km/h = 1000/3600 m/s, so a speed in km/h is divided by 3.6 to get m/s.\n\nExample: Correct 4.5678 to three significant figures: The options are 4.56, 4.57, 4.58 and 4.568. The significant figures are 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8, and we must stop after the third, which is the 6. The next digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so the 6 is rounded up to 7 and the answer is 4.57. Option 4.56 simply drops the 7, 8 and truncates instead of rounding, so it understates the value. Option 4.58 rounds twice, pushing the 7 up as well, which changes a number you were asked to correct to three figures. Option 4.568 keeps four figures and is not a correction to three at all.\n\nAlso: A speed of 72 km/h is equal to: The options are 2 m/s, 20 m/s, 200 m/s and 2.7 m/s. 20 m/s is correct, because 1 km/h means 1000 m in 3600 s, so every value in km/h is divided by 3.6 to reach m/s, and 72 / 3.6 = 20. 200 m/s comes from multiplying by 3.6 instead of dividing, or from moving the decimal one place to the right, and 2 m/s is that same error made twice. 2.7 m/s is the odd one out, about a seventh of the true value, and it appears when 3.6 is mis-remembered as a factor of roughly 27, so divide rather than guess.\n\nAnother: A vernier caliper has 10 vernier scale divisions spanning 9 main scale divisions of 1 mm each. Its least count is: The options are 0.01 mm, 1 mm, 0.5 mm and 0.1 mm. 0.1 mm is correct, because the least count is one main scale division minus one vernier scale division, and since 10 vernier divisions span 9 main divisions, one vernier division is 9/10 of a millimetre, so the least count is 1 - 0.9 = 0.1 mm. 0.01 mm is the answer for the common direct vernier with 10 divisions spanning 9 mm read as a full ratio of 1 to 100, and it is the trap for anyone who assumes every vernier is the same. 1 mm is just one main scale division with the vernier ignored, so the caliper would gain no accuracy at all. 0.5 mm assumes the divisions are in a 1 to 2 ratio, which is not what the question describes.\n\nWatch out: counting the leading zeros in 0.00450, which gives four or five instead of three, and treating the final zero as insignificant. That trailing zero sits right next to the 5, so it is a significant figure and is kept.\n\nIn short: a number alone is not a measurement, so always attach the unit, the dimension and the correct number of significant figures.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Linear motion',
    qt: ['Motion and forces'],
    title: 'Acceleration, graphs and projectiles',
    body: 'Linear motion covers the equations of motion, the slope and area of the two graphs you must know, and projectile motion, and it forms the largest block of marks in the Physics paper. Speed, velocity and acceleration are distinguished carefully, and almost every numerical question can be solved with one of three equations.\n\nHere\'s the idea: for uniform acceleration the three quantities u, a, t, v and s are locked together by v = u + at, s = ut + 1/2 at^2 and v^2 = u + 2as, so you only need to recognise which pair of them the question gives you and which one it asks for.\n\nStep 1 - List what you are given and what you want, writing u, a, t, v and s explicitly, and remember that from rest means u = 0 while stopping means the final v = 0.\nStep 2 - Pick the equation that contains exactly the quantities you have plus the one you need, rather than the one you remember best.\nStep 3 - Substitute with g = 10 m/s^2, square the speed or time rather than doubling it, and keep the units on every line.\nStep 4 - Sanity check the direction of the answer: a body thrown up slows to zero and then comes down, and a graph question is read from slope for distance-time graphs and from area for velocity-time graphs.\n\nExample: A runner covers 100 m in 10 s and another 100 m in 20 s. The average speed for the whole journey is: The options are 15 m/s, 5 m/s, 6.7 m/s and 10 m/s. Average speed is total distance divided by total time, never the average of the two separate speeds. Total distance is 100 + 100 = 200 m and total time is 10 + 20 = 30 s, so the average speed is 200 / 30 = 6.67 m/s, that is 6.7 m/s. Option 10 m/s is the speed of the first half alone, option 5 m/s is the speed of the second half alone, and option 15 m/s comes from adding 10 + 5 instead of averaging them.\n\nAlso: A stone is thrown vertically upward at 30 m/s. The maximum height reached (take g = 10 m/s^2) is: The options are 90 m, 15 m, 30 m and 45 m. 45 m is correct, because the stone leaves the hand and slows under gravity until its velocity is zero at the top, so with v^2 = u^2 - 2gh the height is (30 x 30) / (2 x 10) = 900 / 20 = 45 m. 90 m is the tempting one, and it comes from u^2 / g, that is 900 / 10, which forgets the 2 in the equation of motion. 30 m is the launch speed reused as a distance, which is a different quantity. 15 m is half of that, so the two wrong numeric answers are the single-step and the half-step slips, and neither of them uses the final velocity of zero.\n\nAnother: The area under a velocity-time graph represents: The options are Momentum, Acceleration, Distance and Displacement. Displacement is correct, because the area of each thin strip is velocity multiplied by the time interval, which is exactly the distance covered in that strip, and adding the strips gives the distance travelled, which is the displacement here because the body moves in a straight line. Momentum is the trap that makes this question worth thinking about: the area does equal the change in momentum, because momentum is mass multiplied by velocity, but momentum itself is a single value while the area is a total, so the area gives the change, not the momentum. Distance is a near miss, being equal to displacement only for straight-line motion with no reversal. Acceleration is the slope of the graph, not the area, and confusing the two is the single most common fault on graph questions.\n\nWatch out: adding the two stage speeds, or dividing the distance of one stage by the time of the other. Average speed is always total distance over total time, so if the two stages take equal times the answer is exactly the average of the speeds, but never if the times differ.\n\nIn short: total distance over total time, and choose the one equation of motion that contains your unknowns.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Forces',
    qt: ['Motion and forces', 'Work energy and power'],
    title: 'Resultants, friction and pressure',
    body: 'Forces is the second largest block in the Physics paper, covering Newton laws, friction, pressure, moments, momentum, upthrust and circular motion. The ideas are tightly linked: pressure is a force spread over an area, friction is a force that depends on the normal reaction, and centripetal force is the net force pointing at the centre of a circle.\n\nHere\'s the idea: every force question is Newton\'s second law in disguise, so find the resultant of the forces first, and then apply F = ma with the mass expressed in kilograms and the acceleration in m/s^2.\n\nStep 1 - Draw a free body diagram and list every force on the body, including weight, normal reaction, upthrust and friction, so that nothing is silently missing.\nStep 2 - Resolve into perpendicular directions, or work along the direction of motion if it is a straight line, and add the components with their signs.\nStep 3 - Apply the matching formula: F = ma, pressure = force / area, liquid pressure = density x g x depth, moment = force x perpendicular distance, and limiting friction F = mu x R.\nStep 4 - Use P = mg for the weight, remembering that R is the normal reaction and is not automatically equal to the weight, and check that the answer carries a unit and the right size.\n\nExample: A body of mass 5 kg rests on a rough horizontal surface. If the coefficient of friction is 0.4 and g = 10 m/s^2, the minimum force needed to just move it is: The options are 50 N, 25 N, 10 N and 20 N. The pull must just balance the limiting friction, so F = mu x R. On a horizontal surface the normal reaction R equals the weight mg = 5 x 10 = 50 N, hence F = 0.4 x 50 = 20 N. Option 50 N is mg, the weight of the body, which is what you get if you forget friction altogether. Option 10 N uses mu x g = 0.4 x 10 and leaves the 5 kg mass out. Option 25 N comes from adding the 5 kg to the 20 N instead of multiplying by it.\n\nAlso: A car of mass 1500 kg moving at 20 m/s is brought to rest in 10 s. The magnitude of the average braking force is: The options are 300 N, 15000 N, 3000 N and 1500 N. 3000 N is correct, because the force is the rate of change of momentum, and the momentum goes from 1500 x 20 = 30000 kg m/s down to zero, so F = 30000 / 10 = 3000 N. You can reach the same figure the long way round by finding the deceleration first, a = (0 - 20) / 10 = -2 m/s^2, and then using F = ma = 1500 x 2 = 3000 N. 15000 N is what you get from dividing the momentum by 2 instead of by the 10 s given, and it is the trap for a candidate who halves the time because 10 looks like a round number. 1500 N is the mass figure copied straight out of the question with no calculation done to it. 300 N is a decimal slip on the same calculation.\n\nAnother: A body of mass 2 kg moves in a circle of radius 5 m at 10 m/s. The centripetal force required is: The options are 4 N, 40 N, 20 N and 100 N. 40 N is correct, because the speed is fixed, so all the acceleration is centripetal and directed at the centre, which makes F = mv^2 / r = 2 x 100 / 5 = 40 N. Work in the order given, squaring the speed before dividing by the radius, and the answer arrives in one line. 20 N is 100 / 5, the centripetal acceleration 20 m/s^2 given a mass but no force, so it is the option you pick if you stop one step short. 100 N is the square of the speed on its own, which has dropped both the mass and the radius. 4 N is mv / r, which uses the speed where its square belongs. Since all four options are near neighbours, working the substitution aloud once is enough to keep your place.\n\nWatch out: assuming the normal reaction equals the weight when the question gives an accelerating or angled situation, and forgetting that the friction force is the force needed to just move the body, so the answer is the limiting friction and not the motion.\n\nIn short: find every force, resolve, then F = ma with the mass in kilograms and the acceleration in m/s^2.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Work energy and power',
    title: 'Work, energy, power and machines',
    body: 'Work, energy and power are the questions that ask what has been transferred and how fast, and they cover work done, kinetic and potential energy, conservation of energy, power, efficiency, Hooke law and elasticity. Many of them are a single substitution once you have identified which quantity the question wants.\n\nHere\'s the idea: work is force multiplied by distance measured along the force, energy is the capacity to do that work, and power is the rate at which the work is done, so the three sit in a chain of W divided by t.\n\nStep 1 - Identify the quantity asked for: work needs F and s, kinetic energy needs m and v, potential energy needs m, g and h, and power needs work and time.\nStep 2 - Substitute with everything in SI units, so masses in kilograms, distances in metres and times in seconds, and use g = 10 m/s^2.\nStep 3 - For power, remember that work must be found first: 500 N lifted through 3 m is 1500 J, and 1500 J in 10 s is 150 W.\nStep 4 - For a machine, divide useful output by input: efficiency = useful work / energy in x 100, which also equals mechanical advantage / velocity ratio x 100.\n\nExample: An engine lifts a load of 500 N through a height of 3 m in 10 s. The power developed is: The options are 1.5 W, 15 W, 150 W and 1500 W. The work done against gravity is force x distance = 500 x 3 = 1500 J, and power is the rate of doing work, so P = 1500 / 10 = 150 W. Option 1500 W is the work in joules with no division by time, and it is the single most common slip in this topic. Option 15 W divides by 100 instead of by 10, or moves the decimal once too far. Option 1.5 W moves it twice.\n\nAlso: A spring of force constant 200 N/m is stretched by 0.15 m. The energy stored in it is: The options are 2.25 J, 30 J, 3 J and 0.75 J. 2.25 J is correct, because the elastic potential energy is half the force constant times the square of the extension, so E = 0.5 x 200 x 0.15^2 = 100 x 0.0225 = 2.25 J. The two operations to watch are the halving and the squaring, and both are needed. 30 J is the spring force multiplied by the extension, since 200 x 0.15 = 30, which is kx in newtons read as an energy; that is the answer for a spring obeying Hooke law over a single extension, not the stored energy. 3 J and 0.75 J are the values produced by mishandling the 0.5 and the square, and neither step appears anywhere in the correct expression.\n\nAnother: A machine has a velocity ratio of 5 and an efficiency of 80%. Its mechanical advantage is: The options are 5, 2, 4 and 1.25. 4 is correct, because a machine is never perfect, so the mechanical advantage is the velocity ratio scaled by the efficiency, and MA = efficiency x VR / 100 = 80 x 5 / 100 = 4. The efficiency must enter as a percentage and be divided by 100 exactly once. 5 is the velocity ratio on its own, and it is the tempting choice for anyone who treats an 80 per cent efficient machine as fully efficient. 1.25 is 5 divided by 4, which comes from reading 80 per cent as a factor of 4 instead of as a percentage, so the conversion is inverted. 2 is the correct answer halved once more, a decimal slip on a small number.\n\nWatch out: reporting energy in joules when the question asked for power in watts. Power always carries a division by time, and 1 W = 1 J/s, while the answers in the options differ only by a factor of 10, which tells you exactly where the decimal point went wrong.\n\nIn short: work is force times distance, energy is what is stored, and power is that energy divided by time.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Heat and temperature',
    qt: ['Heat and thermodynamics'],
    title: 'Temperature, heat and gas laws',
    body: 'Heat and temperature questions mix definitions with two formula families: Q = mc delta T for a change of temperature and Q = mL for a change of state, together with the transfer of heat and the gas laws. Temperature scales and expansion questions are definition marks and appear in the first section, while the calculations run through the middle of the paper.\n\nHere\'s the idea: temperature measures average kinetic energy while heat measures energy transferred, so a rise in temperature needs mc delta T and a melting or boiling needs mL with no change of temperature at all.\n\nStep 1 - Identify the process: warming or cooling uses Q = mc delta T, melting or boiling uses Q = mL, and a temperature conversion uses K = degrees Celsius + 273.\nStep 2 - Substitute in SI units with c in J/kg K and L in J/kg, so a mass in kilograms, and remember the delta T is a difference so it may be negative.\nStep 3 - For the gas laws convert every temperature to kelvin first, then choose P1/T1 = P2/T2 at constant volume, V1/T1 = V2/T2 at constant pressure, or P1V1 = P2V2 at constant temperature.\nStep 4 - Check the direction: heating a gas at constant pressure must increase its volume, and heating a gas at constant volume must increase its pressure.\n\nExample: The quantity of heat needed to raise the temperature of 2 kg of water through 10 degrees C (specific heat capacity 4200 J/kg K) is: The options are 8400 J, 4200 J, 84000 J and 21000 J. Here Q = mc delta T = 2 x 4200 x 10 = 84000 J. Option 8400 J is the calculation for 1 kg, so it forgets the mass and leaves out the factor of 2. Option 4200 J is just c on its own, using delta T = 1. Option 21000 J is what you get for 1 kg of a substance whose specific heat capacity is 2100 J/kg K, so it forgets the 2 kg and borrows the wrong constant.\n\nAlso: A steel rod 2.0 m long is heated through 50 K. If its coefficient of linear expansion is 1.2 x 10^-5 K^-1, the increase in length is: The options are 12 mm, 0.12 mm, 0.012 mm and 1.2 mm. 1.2 mm is correct, because the increase in length is the coefficient of linear expansion times the original length times the temperature rise, so dL = 1.2 x 10^-5 x 2.0 x 50 = 1.2 x 10^-3 m, and that is 1.2 mm once the answer is in millimetres. The length starts in metres and the options are in millimetres, so the conversion at the end is the whole difficulty of the question. 12 mm is the same number with the decimal point moved one place to the right, 0.12 mm and 0.012 mm are it moved one and two places to the left, and a rise in temperature always makes a rod longer, never shorter, which rules out any negative reading.\n\nAnother: A gas occupies 0.50 m^3 at a pressure of 200 kPa. Its volume at 400 kPa, temperature kept constant, is: The options are 1.00 m^3, 0.50 m^3, 0.25 m^3 and 0.10 m^3. 0.25 m^3 is correct, because at constant temperature pressure and volume are inversely proportional, so P1V1 = P2V2 gives 200 x 0.5 = 400 x V2 and V2 = 100 / 400 = 0.25 m^3. The pressure has doubled, so the volume must halve, and the result is a quarter of the original figure of 0.50. 1.00 m^3 doubles the volume instead of halving it, and it is the trap for anyone who treats the two as moving in step. 0.50 m^3 is the volume unchanged, as if the second pressure were a distraction. 0.10 m^3 halves twice, which would need the pressure to rise fourfold.\n\nWatch out: dropping the mass when the specific heat capacity is used, and using degrees Celsius in a gas law. For a gas at constant volume with 100 kPa at 27 degrees C, the pressure at 127 degrees C is 100 x 400 / 300 = 133.3 kPa, and using 27 and 127 directly gives a nonsense answer.\n\nIn short: mc delta T for a temperature change, mL for a change of state, and kelvin in every gas law.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Waves',
    qt: ['Waves and sound'],
    title: 'Wave language and superposition',
    body: 'Wave questions test the language of waves, the wave equation, superposition and diffraction, and they are the easiest marks in the Physics paper once the definitions are secure. A wave moves energy without carrying matter with it, and almost every numerical question is v = f x wavelength rearranged.\n\nHere\'s the idea: frequency, wavelength and speed are locked by speed = frequency x wavelength, and when a wave changes medium the frequency is fixed by the source while the speed and the wavelength change together.\n\nStep 1 - Decide which of the three you are given and which you want, then rearrange v = f x wavelength into the form you need.\nStep 2 - Convert periods to frequencies with f = 1/T before substituting, so 0.05 s becomes 20 Hz.\nStep 3 - Identify the type of wave: transverse waves vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel as light does, longitudinal waves vibrate along it as sound does, and only electromagnetic waves cross a vacuum.\nStep 4 - Read the definitions exactly, because they are asked verbatim: wavelength is the distance between two consecutive points in phase, frequency counts complete waves per second, and superposition says the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum.\n\nExample: A wave has a frequency of 50 Hz and a wavelength of 4 m. Its speed is: The options are 5 m/s, 20 m/s, 12.5 m/s and 200 m/s. The wave equation gives speed = frequency x wavelength = 50 x 4 = 200 m/s. Option 12.5 m/s is 50 / 4, so it divides where it should multiply, and it is the classic trap in this question. Option 20 m/s uses a frequency of 5 Hz instead of 50 Hz. Option 5 m/s comes from dividing 20 by 4 as well.\n\nAlso: When a wave passes from air into glass, its frequency: The options are Increases, Decreases, Remains constant and Becomes zero. Remains constant is correct, because the frequency is fixed by the source that sets the vibrations, so every wave leaving the source has the same frequency whatever medium it then travels through. What does change is the speed, which rises sharply in glass, and the wavelength, which must rise in the same proportion so that speed = frequency x wavelength still holds. Increases is the tempting wrong answer, because the wave does move faster in glass, but a faster wave carrying more cycles past a point in the same second would be a different note, not the same one bent. Decreases has the same flaw. Becomes zero confuses refraction with blocking, and some of the wave is reflected at the boundary, not all of it stopped.\n\nAnother: The wavelength of a sound wave of frequency 170 Hz travelling at 340 m/s is: The options are 0.5 m, 1 m, 2 m and 170 m. 2 m is correct, because the wave equation speed = frequency x wavelength rearranges to wavelength = speed / frequency, so 340 / 170 = 2 m. Sanity check it: a frequency of 170 Hz and a speed of 340 m/s mean two complete waves every metre, which is a wavelength of 2 m. 170 m is the frequency read straight across as a wavelength, so it fails the check immediately. 1 m comes from dividing 170 by 170 instead of 340 by 170, that is using the frequency twice. 0.5 m halves the correct answer, and halving a wavelength without halving the speed or doubling the frequency cannot be right.\n\nWatch out: dividing instead of multiplying, and forgetting that frequency never changes when a wave crosses a boundary. When a wave passes from air into glass the frequency stays fixed while the speed increases, so the wavelength must increase in the same proportion.\n\nIn short: speed = frequency x wavelength, frequency is fixed by the source, and wavelength changes with the speed.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Sound',
    qt: ['Waves and sound'],
    title: 'Echoes, beats and resonance',
    body: 'Sound is the wave topic that comes with a medium requirement, so its questions mix wave ideas with applications: echoes and SONAR, beats, resonance, pitch, loudness and quality, and the audible range. Almost all of it is conceptual, and the echo question is the one calculation you must get exactly right.\n\nHere\'s the idea: sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave, so it needs matter to travel through and it travels fastest in solids and slowest in gases, and any echo calculation must count the round trip, not the one-way journey.\n\nStep 1 - Fix the speed of sound in air at about 340 m/s, and remember that it is a longitudinal wave needing a medium, which is why it cannot cross a vacuum.\nStep 2 - For an echo, multiply speed by the elapsed time to get the total distance travelled, then halve it to get the distance to the reflecting surface.\nStep 3 - Separate the three hearing ideas: pitch comes from frequency, loudness comes from amplitude, and quality or timbre tells two instruments playing the same note apart.\nStep 4 - Apply the special facts: beat frequency is the difference of the two frequencies, resonance occurs when the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, and intensity obeys the inverse square law.\n\nExample: An observer hears an echo 2 s after shouting at a cliff. The distance of the cliff (speed of sound = 340 m/s) is: The options are 680 m, 340 m, 170 m and 34 m. In 2 s the sound travels 340 x 2 = 680 m, but that is the round trip, out to the cliff and back to the observer. The one-way distance is therefore 680 / 2 = 340 m. Option 680 m forgets the round trip and is the standard trap. Option 170 m divides by 4, halving twice. Option 34 m moves the decimal one place instead of dividing.\n\nAlso: Two tuning forks vibrate at 250 Hz and 252 Hz. The beat frequency heard is: The options are 0.5 Hz, 251 Hz, 502 Hz and 2 Hz. 2 Hz is correct, because beats are produced by the interference of two waves of slightly different frequency, and the number of beats heard each second is the difference between them, so 252 - 250 = 2 Hz. 251 Hz is the average, and it describes the pitch you actually hear rather than the pulsing on and off that you hear it with, which is why it is such a good distractor. 502 Hz is the sum, produced by adding the two frequencies instead of subtracting them. 0.5 Hz halves the difference, so the beats would be eight times too slow.\n\nAnother: The intensity of sound obeys the inverse square law. When the distance from the source is doubled, the intensity becomes: The options are Double, Zero, Unchanged and One quarter. One quarter is correct, because an inverse square law means intensity varies as 1 over the square of the distance, so doubling the distance multiplies the denominator by 2 x 2 = 4 and the intensity falls to a quarter of its former value. This is why a faint sound becomes very faint quickly as you walk away from it, and it applies to any source radiating in every direction. Double reads the law as if it were an inverse law, halving the intensity per doubling, and is the most common misreading. Unchanged ignores the distance altogether. Zero is wrong because the sound is still there, just spread over a much larger sphere.\n\nWatch out: reporting the total distance travelled by the sound rather than the distance of the reflecting surface. The same halving applies to every echo, SONAR and depth question, and 2 seconds is also the minimum delay before the ear distinguishes an echo as a separate sound.\n\nIn short: sound needs a medium, an echo doubles the journey, and pitch is frequency while loudness is amplitude.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Light and optics',
    qt: ['Optics'],
    title: 'Reflection, refraction and lenses',
    body: 'Light and optics covers reflection, refraction, refractive index, critical angle and total internal reflection, plane and spherical mirrors, lenses, optical defects and dispersion. Questions mix definitions, sign-free calculations such as lens power, and reasoning about where an image forms, and the units of focal length are the usual trap.\n\nHere\'s the idea: lenses and mirrors are described by two numbers, the focal length and the power, and the power is simply the focal length in metres turned upside down, so a short focal length always means a powerful lens.\n\nStep 1 - Identify whether the object or the medium is denser, because light bends towards the normal when going from rarer to denser and away from the normal in the reverse direction.\nStep 2 - For refractive index use n = c / v with c = 3 x 10^8 m/s, and for the critical angle use sin C = 1 / n.\nStep 3 - For a lens convert the focal length to metres before dividing: power P = 1 / f in dioptres, so 20 cm becomes 0.2 m and gives 5 D.\nStep 4 - Check the type of image against the device: a plane mirror gives a virtual, erect, same-size image, a real image needs a convex lens or a concave mirror, and short sight needs a concave lens while long sight needs a convex one.\n\nExample: A convex lens of focal length 20 cm has a power of: The options are 0.2 D, 2 D, 5 D and 20 D. Power is the reciprocal of the focal length expressed in metres, so 20 cm = 0.2 m and P = 1 / 0.2 = 5 D. Option 0.2 D is simply the focal length in metres with no division, and it is the trap for anyone who forgets the reciprocal. Option 2 D results from reading 20 cm as 0.5 m. Option 20 D leaves the focal length in centimetres.\n\nAlso: A concave mirror of focal length 10 cm has an object placed 30 cm in front of it. The image is formed: The options are 15 cm in front of the mirror, 15 cm behind the mirror, 30 cm in front of the mirror and 7.5 cm in front of the mirror. 15 cm in front of the mirror is correct, because with real quantities taken as positive the mirror formula gives 1/f = 1/u + 1/v, so 1/10 = 1/30 + 1/v and 1/v = 1/10 - 1/30 = 2/30 = 1/15, giving v = 15 cm. A positive image distance is a real image, and a real image from a concave mirror always lies in front of the mirror, so the position is settled as soon as the arithmetic is. 15 cm behind the mirror has the right size of number and the wrong side, and it is only possible for a concave mirror when the object is inside the focal length, which 30 cm is not. 7.5 cm halves the answer, usually by halving the 1/v you have just found. 30 cm in front assumes the image distance equals the object distance, which is a coincidence of the 2f case and not a rule.\n\nAnother: The critical angle for a medium of refractive index 1.414 is approximately: The options are 30 degrees, 60 degrees, 90 degrees and 45 degrees. 45 degrees is correct, because the critical angle is defined by a ray leaving the denser medium along the surface, so the angle of refraction is 90 degrees and the refractive index relationship sin C = 1 over n gives sin C = 1 / 1.414 = 0.707, and the angle whose sine is 0.707 is 45 degrees. Notice that 1.414 is 2 to the power of one half, which is why the numbers are chosen so neatly, so keep the sine and the inverse side straight. 90 degrees is the limit when there is no bending at all, that is when the refractive index is 1. 60 degrees uses a sine of 0.866, which belongs to a refractive index of about 1.15. 30 degrees is what you get by subtracting 1 from the refractive index instead of dividing into it.\n\nWatch out: leaving the focal length in centimetres inside the formula, which is wrong by a factor of 100. On the same idea, two lenses of 2 D and 3 D in contact give 5 D because powers simply add, whereas focal lengths must be combined as 1/f = 1/f1 + 1/f2.\n\nIn short: convert the focal length to metres, invert it for power, and let the denser or rarer medium decide which way the ray bends.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Electricity',
    qt: ['Electricity and magnetism'],
    title: 'Current, resistance and circuit rules',
    body: 'Electricity questions cover current, potential difference, resistance, series and parallel arrangements, electrical power and energy, and the purpose of fuses. The definitions are easy marks, while the series and parallel rules account for a good share of the numerical questions in the second half of the paper.\n\nHere\'s the idea: everything follows from Ohm law V = IR together with two arrangement rules, that series resistances add and that parallel resistances combine so the result is always smaller than the smallest branch.\n\nStep 1 - Write the quantity you want and rearrange Ohm law for it: V = IR, I = V / R, R = V / I.\nStep 2 - Decide whether the components are in series or in parallel, then apply the rule: in series R = R1 + R2 + R3 with the same current everywhere, in parallel 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2 with the same voltage across each branch.\nStep 3 - For power use P = VI = I^2 R = V^2 / R, and for energy use energy = power x time, converting hours to seconds or using watts and kilowatt hours as the units require.\nStep 4 - Sanity check the size: a parallel combination must be less than either branch, a series combination must be greater, and a fuse rating must sit just above the normal current from P = VI.\n\nExample: The effective resistance of a 2 Ohm resistor connected in parallel with a 3 Ohm resistor is: The options are 5 Ohm, 1.2 Ohm, 6 Ohm and 0.6 Ohm. Adding the reciprocals gives 1/R = 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6, so R = 6 / 5 = 1.2 Ohm. Equivalently the product over the sum is 2 x 3 / (2 + 3) = 6 / 5 = 1.2 Ohm. Option 5 Ohm is the series answer, obtained by adding, and it is the trap for anyone who ignores the word parallel. Option 6 Ohm is the product 2 x 3, which is larger than either resistor and so impossible for a parallel pair. Option 0.6 Ohm comes from dividing 6 by 10 instead of by 5.\n\nAlso: A 60 W lamp is used for 2 hours. The energy it consumes is: The options are 120 kWh, 0.06 kWh, 0.3 kWh and 0.12 kWh. 0.12 kWh is correct, because energy is power multiplied by time, so 60 W x 2 h = 120 Wh, and a kilowatt hour is 1000 Wh, so 120 Wh is 0.12 kWh. The whole question turns on dividing by 1000 exactly once at the end. 120 kWh is the number you have before the conversion and is the trap for anyone who reads watts as kilowatts straight away, and it is also the figure in joules multiplied wrongly, since 120 Wh is 432000 J. 0.06 kWh and 0.3 kWh are the two decimal slips that bracket the correct answer once you start moving the point, so check the digit count rather than the digit.\n\nAnother: A current of 3 A flows through a 2 Ohm resistor. The power dissipated as heat is: The options are 6 W, 9 W, 1.5 W and 18 W. 18 W is correct, because power dissipated in a resistor is the square of the current times the resistance, so P = I^2 R = 3 x 3 x 2 = 18 W, and you can confirm it with P = V^2 / R once you have the voltage. 6 W is the potential difference across the resistor, since V = IR = 3 x 2 = 6 V, and a voltage in volts is not a power in watts, so that option is a genuine quantity from this question and still the wrong one. 9 W is I^2 with the resistance left out. 1.5 W is a decimal slip.\n\nWatch out: adding instead of combining in parallel, and quoting a parallel result that is larger than the smallest branch, which is impossible because the branches share the current. Mixing series and parallel rules inside one question is the usual cause.\n\nIn short: series adds and parallel divides reciprocally, then use V = IR and P = VI with everything in volts, amperes and ohms.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Magnetism',
    qt: ['Electricity and magnetism'],
    title: 'Fields, motors and induction',
    body: 'Magnetism covers magnetic field patterns, the motor and generator effects, electromagnetic induction, the transformer, and the choice of materials for cores. The questions are mostly definition and reasoning, with a small number of force and transformer calculations that follow from two formulas.\n\nHere\'s the idea: the motor effect turns current in a field into force and the generator effect turns motion in a field into current, and both are remembered by their hand rules rather than by any calculation.\n\nStep 1 - Fix the field pattern: outside a bar magnet the lines run from north to south, inside the magnet from south to north, a straight current gives circular field lines around itself, and a long solenoid gives a uniform field along its axis.\nStep 2 - Use the correct hand rule: Fleming left-hand rule gives the direction of force in a motor, and Fleming right-hand rule gives the direction of induced current in a generator or dynamo.\nStep 3 - Calculate the motor force with F = BIL when the conductor is at right angles to the field, multiplying flux density, current and length in that order.\nStep 4 - Use the transformer ratio Vs/Vp = Ns/Np, and choose soft iron for electromagnet cores because it magnetises and demagnetises easily, while steel keeps its magnetism for permanent magnets.\n\nExample: A conductor of length 0.2 m carrying a current of 4 A is placed at right angles to a uniform field of flux density 0.5 T. The force on it is: The options are 0.4 N, 4 N, 0.04 N and 2 N. At right angles F = BIL = 0.5 x 4 x 0.2 = 0.4 N, since 4 x 0.2 = 0.8 and 0.5 x 0.8 = 0.4. Option 2 N is B x I on its own, 0.5 x 4 = 2, with the length left out. Option 4 N is the current on its own, and option 0.04 N comes from dividing by ten where you should multiply.\n\nAlso: A transformer has 100 turns in its primary coil and 500 turns in its secondary coil. If the primary is supplied with 60 V, the secondary voltage is: The options are 300 V, 12 V, 60 V and 30 V. 300 V is correct, because a transformer obeys Vs over Vp equals Ns over Np, and the turns ratio here is 500 over 100, which is 5, so the secondary voltage is 5 x 60 = 300 V. Ask yourself whether the secondary coil has more turns than the primary, and if it does the step-up transformer must raise the voltage, which fixes the direction of the calculation. 12 V is the ratio applied the wrong way round, 60 V assumes a 1 to 1 turns ratio, and 30 V halves the primary voltage, which corresponds to halving the turns rather than multiplying by five.\n\nAnother: The magnetic field inside a long solenoid carrying a steady current is: The options are Zero, Circular, Non-uniform and Uniform and parallel to the axis. Uniform and parallel to the axis is correct, because the field of a long straight current-carrying coil is made up of circular loops round every turn, and because the turns are packed closely along a straight axis those loops reinforce each other inside the coil and almost cancel outside it, leaving a nearly uniform field along the axis. Zero is wrong for the same reason a bar magnet is not zero inside, and in fact the interior of a solenoid is where the field is strongest, which is why electromagnets are built this way. Circular describes the field round a single straight wire, not inside a coil. Non-uniform describes the field inside a bar magnet or near a single conductor, where the lines spread out.\n\nWatch out: dropping a factor of ten by leaving the length in centimetres, and using the right-hand rule for a motor. Left hand is for motors where current in a field gives force; right hand is for generators where motion in a field gives current.\n\nIn short: field lines run north to south outside the magnet, left hand gives motor force, right hand gives generator current.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Nuclear physics',
    qt: ['Radiation'],
    title: 'Radioactivity, half-life and energy',
    body: 'Nuclear physics covers the structure of the atom, the three types of radiation, half-life and the uses of isotopes, and it also includes fission, fusion and nuclear energy. Most of the marks are for precise definitions and for knowing which property belongs to which type of radiation.\n\nHere\'s the idea: radioactive decay is random and spontaneous at the level of each nucleus, so you cannot predict when one particular nucleus will decay, but you can predict how the total count falls over many half-lives because the activity halves each time.\n\nStep 1 - Name the quantities: the atomic number counts protons and the mass number counts protons plus neutrons, so neutrons = mass number minus atomic number.\nStep 2 - Match each radiation to its properties: alpha particles are helium nuclei, strongly ionising but stopped by paper; beta particles are fast electrons, stopped by a few millimetres of aluminium; gamma rays are uncharged electromagnetic radiation, stopped only by thick lead.\nStep 3 - Use the half-life by halving the remaining amount each time: an 80 g sample is 40 g after one half-life, 20 g after two, 10 g after three and 5 g after four, and it never reaches zero.\nStep 4 - Separate the two nuclear reactions: fission splits a heavy nucleus such as uranium and sustains a chain reaction, while fusion joins light nuclei as in the Sun and needs an extremely high temperature.\n\nExample: The half-life of a radioactive substance is the time taken for: The options are half of the nuclei present to decay, its activity to fall to zero, all of its nuclei to decay, and its nuclei to double in number. The correct one is half of the nuclei present to decay, because after each half-life the remaining amount is divided by two, so a sample that is halved twice has a quarter left and one that is halved three times has an eighth left, never nothing at all. Its activity to fall to zero is wrong because the activity falls by half at every step and so approaches zero without reaching it. All of its nuclei to decay is wrong on the same grounds, since even after four half-lives one sixteenth still remains. Its nuclei to double in number describes the opposite process, which is what happens in a fusion chain rather than in decay.\n\nAlso: An alpha particle is: The options are A fast-moving electron, A helium nucleus, An electromagnetic ray and A hydrogen nucleus. A helium nucleus is correct, because an alpha particle is two protons and two neutrons bound together, which is the nucleus of a helium-4 atom, with a mass number of 4 and a charge of plus 2. That double charge is why it ionises air very strongly and yet is stopped by a sheet of paper. A fast-moving electron is exactly what a beta particle is, so this option is the confusion every candidate has to shake off. An electromagnetic ray is a gamma ray, which is uncharged and needs thick lead to stop. A hydrogen nucleus is a single proton, which is what happens after an alpha particle has lost its two electrons and left the atom.\n\nAnother: Radioactive decay of a given nucleus is: The options are Predictable for that individual nucleus, Caused by changes in temperature, Random and spontaneous and Reversible at will. Random and spontaneous is correct, because the decay of a particular nucleus depends on quantum probability rather than on anything measurable beforehand, so you cannot say when or whether any one nucleus will decay, only that a large sample falls off in a predictable way with a fixed half-life. Predictable for that individual nucleus is the wrong half of the truth, because the half-life predicts the behaviour of the whole sample, not the fate of one nucleus, and that distinction is the heart of the topic. Caused by changes in temperature confuses a real effect, since the decay rate does rise slightly with temperature, with the cause, which is nuclear instability in the nucleus itself and not a change in the surroundings. Reversible at will is impossible, because the change is written into the nucleus.\n\nWatch out: thinking decay can be sped up or slowed down. No change of temperature, pressure or chemical combination affects it, and the activity, not the time, is halved each half-life.\n\nIn short: alpha is a helium nucleus, beta an electron, gamma an electromagnetic ray, and half the nuclei decay every half-life without ever reaching zero.'
  },
  {
    subject: 'Physics',
    type: 'note',
    topic: 'Electronics',
    qt: ['Electronics'],
    title: 'Semiconductors, diodes and logic gates',
    body: 'Electronics covers semiconductor materials, doping to produce n-type and p-type material, the p-n junction diode, the transistor, rectification, sensors, logic gates and the use of capacitors for smoothing. It is the newest topic on the syllabus and its questions are short recall or simple reasoning.\n\nHere\'s the idea: a semiconductor conducts between an insulator and a metal, and doping decides whether the free charge carriers are electrons or holes, while a diode uses that two-type structure to let current pass in one direction only.\n\nStep 1 - Classify the impurity: a pentavalent impurity has five outer electrons and donates a free electron, giving n-type material, while a trivalent impurity has three outer electrons and leaves a hole, giving p-type material.\nStep 2 - Recall the rest of the building blocks: the semiconductor diode conducts in one direction only, rectification is the conversion of AC into DC, and a transistor has three terminals and works as an amplifier or a switch.\nStep 3 - Work through the logic gates: an OR gate gives output 1 when at least one input is 1, an AND gate gives output 1 only when all inputs are 1, and a NOT gate always gives the opposite of its input.\nStep 4 - Use the sensors: the resistance of an LDR falls as light intensity increases and the resistance of a thermistor falls as temperature rises, so either can switch a lamp on automatically when it gets dark.\n\nExample: The majority charge carriers in a p-type semiconductor are: The options are electrons, protons, neutrons and holes. The answer is holes. Silicon has four outer electrons, and doping it with a trivalent impurity such as indium or aluminium leaves one electron short at each doped site, and each of these vacancies behaves as a mobile positive carrier called a hole. Electrons are the majority carriers only in n-type material made with a pentavalent impurity. Protons and neutrons are nuclear particles that are not free to move through the lattice, and a semiconductor is always electrically neutral overall, so there is no excess of either sign, only a difference in which carrier is more numerous.\n\nAlso: An n-type semiconductor is produced by doping pure silicon with a: The options are Trivalent impurity, Pentavalent impurity, Heptavalent impurity and Inert gas impurity. Pentavalent impurity is correct, because silicon has four outer electrons and forms four covalent bonds, so adding an atom from group V such as phosphorus, arsenic or antimony, which brings five outer electrons, leaves four electrons tied up in the lattice and one free electron available to move. The abundance of free electrons is what makes the material n-type, and that is the whole difference between the two types. Trivalent impurity is the mirror-image case, since a group III atom such as indium or aluminium leaves one electron short and so produces holes and p-type material. Heptavalent impurity is not a dopant used in silicon devices. Inert gas impurity forms no bonds at all and does not conduct.\n\nAnother: The output of an AND logic gate is 1 only when: The options are At least one input is 1, All inputs are 0, The two inputs differ and Both inputs are 1. Both inputs are 1 is correct, because an AND gate gives an output of 1 only if every one of its inputs is 1, and gives 0 in every other combination, so 1 and 1 is the single case that lights the output. At least one input is 1 is the definition of an OR gate, and it is the tempting option precisely because an OR gate gives 1 more often than an AND, so candidates who half remember which gate they are on pick it. The two inputs differ describes an exclusive OR gate. All inputs are 0 forces the output to 0 rather than 1, since there is nothing to pass on.\n\nWatch out: confusing the two kinds of doping and the two carriers. N-type means pentavalent and electrons, p-type means trivalent and holes, and the resistance of an LDR falls as the light gets stronger rather than weaker.\n\nIn short: pentavalent gives n-type and electrons, trivalent gives p-type and holes, and the diode passes current one way only.'
  },
  { subject: 'Physics', type: 'sheet', topic: 'Formula sheet', title: 'Formulas worth memorising', body: '- Force: F = ma\n- Work: W = F s cos(theta); 1 J = 1 N m\n- Kinetic energy: KE = 1/2 mv^2; potential energy: PE = mgh\n- Power: P = W/t = Fv, in watts\n- Equations of motion: v = u + at, s = ut + 1/2 at^2, v^2 = u^2 + 2as\n- Projectile range: R = u^2 sin(2 theta)/g; time of flight: t = 2u sin(theta)/g\n- Momentum: p = mv; impulse = Ft = change in momentum\n- Pressure: P = F/A; liquid pressure: p = density x g x h\n- Efficiency: useful output / input x 100 = mechanical advantage / velocity ratio x 100\n- Heat: Q = mc delta T; change of state: Q = mL\n- Wave speed: v = f lambda; period: T = 1/f\n- Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v (real quantities positive)\n- Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u; magnification = image height / object height\n- Lens power: P = 1/f, with f in metres, in dioptres\n- Ohm\'s law: V = IR; series: R = R1 + R2 + R3; parallel: 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2\n- Electrical power: P = VI = I^2 R = V^2/R; energy = power x time\n- Refractive index: n = c/v; critical angle: sin C = 1/n\n- Simple pendulum: T = 2 pi sqrt(L/g); SHM acceleration: a = -(omega^2) x\n- Circular motion: F = mv^2/r, a = v^2/r, time for one revolution = 2 pi r / v\n- Gravitation: F = G m1 m2 / r^2; weight: W = mg\n- Transformer: Vs/Vp = Ns/Np; force on a conductor: F = BIL' },
  { subject: 'Physics', type: 'sheet', topic: 'Units and constants', title: 'SI units and standard values', body: '- SI base units: metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, candela\n- Derived units: force N, work and energy J, power W, pressure Pa, frequency Hz, charge C, resistance ohm, magnetic flux density T, activity Bq, lens power D\n- Speed of light in vacuum: 3 x 10^8 m/s\n- Acceleration due to gravity: g = 10 m/s^2, about 9.8 m/s^2; on the moon g = 1.6 m/s^2\n- Speed of sound in air at room temperature: about 340 m/s; fastest in steel, slowest in air\n- Atmospheric pressure at sea level: about 1 x 10^5 Pa\n- Density of water: 1000 kg/m^3\n- Specific heat capacity of water: 4200 J/kg K; specific latent heat of fusion of ice: 3.36 x 10^5 J/kg\n- Heat capacity of a sample has unit J/K; specific heat capacity has unit J/kg K\n- Normal near point of the human eye: 25 cm; audible range: 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz\n- 1 horsepower = 746 W; 1 kWh = 3.6 x 10^6 J; 1 W = 1 J/s\n- 72 km/h = 20 m/s (divide by 3.6); K = degrees Celsius + 273\n- Escape velocity from the earth: about 11.2 km/s; g is greatest at the poles\n- Weight on the moon is about one sixth of the weight on the earth; mass never changes\n- Carbon-14 half-life: about 5730 years; after 2 half-lives one quarter of the sample remains\n- Useful numbers: pi = 22/7, sin(45 degrees) = 0.707, sqrt(2) = 1.414' },
  { subject: 'Physics', type: 'sheet', topic: 'Definitions', title: 'Definitions to learn word for word', body: '- Scalar: a quantity with magnitude only. Vector: a quantity with magnitude and direction\n- Speed: distance travelled per unit time. Velocity: displacement per unit time\n- Acceleration: the rate of change of velocity, a = (v - u)/t\n- Inertia: the tendency of a body to remain at rest or to continue uniform motion unless a resultant force acts\n- Momentum: mass x velocity, unit kg m/s. Impulse: force x time, unit N s\n- Work: force x distance moved in the direction of the force, unit J\n- Power: the rate of doing work, unit W\n- Efficiency: useful work output divided by energy input, expressed as a percentage\n- Pressure: force per unit area, unit Pa. Density: mass per unit volume\n- Specific heat capacity: heat needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 degree Celsius\n- Specific latent heat: heat needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance with no change in temperature\n- Frequency: complete vibrations per second, unit Hz. Period: time for one vibration\n- Wavelength: distance between two consecutive points in phase. Amplitude: maximum displacement from equilibrium\n- Refractive index: ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed in the medium\n- Critical angle: the angle of incidence in the denser medium that gives an angle of refraction of 90 degrees\n- Magnification: image height divided by object height\n- Half-life: the time taken for half of the nuclei present in a radioactive sample to decay\n- Rectification: the conversion of alternating current into direct current' }
);